"Bounded-orbit lattice representations of finite groups (https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.35191)\nciteturn7academia12 [wordlim: 200] Published: last month; Title: Bounded-orbit lattice representations of finite groups\nAuthors: JiaLi Du, Andrea Lucchini, Joy Morris, Pablo Spiga\nTitle: Bounded-orbit lattice representations of finite groups\nAuthors: JiaLi Du, Andrea Lucchini, Joy Morris, Pablo Spiga\nDate: Mon Sep 28 13:51:16 2026\n\nFor a finite group $G$, let $λ(G)$ denote the minimum number of orbits on the elements of a finite lattice $L$ with $\\operatorname{Aut}(L)\\cong G$. Babai and Goodman conjectured that $λ(G)$ is bounded by an absolute constant. We prove that $λ(G)\\leq 50$ for every finite group $G$, thereby confirming their conjecture. Moreover, the lattice can be chosen to have a regular orbit. The main algebraic ingredient is a decomposition of a generating set of an arbitrary finite $2$-group into an elementary abelian part and two sets in which no quotient of distinct elements is an involution.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nA positivity conjecture for a quotient of $q$-binomial coefficients (https://mat.univie.ac.at/~kratt/artikel/qbinpos.html)\nciteturn7search0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 1.7 years ago; ## Mona Gatzweiler and Christian Krattenthaler\n\n# A positivity conjecture for a quotient of $q$-binomial coefficients ... As a corollary we obtain that a polynomial that is conjectured to be a cyclic sieving polynomial for Kreweras words [S.\n## Mona Gatzweiler and Christian Krattenthaler\n\n# A positivity conjecture for a quotient of $q$-binomial coefficients\n\n### (10 pages)\n\nAbstract. We conjecture that, if the quotient of two q-binomial coefficients with the same top argument is a polynomial, then it has non-negative coefficients. We summarise what is known about the conjecture and prove it in two non-trivial cases. As a corollary we obtain that a polynomial that is conjectured to be a cyclic sieving polynomial for Kreweras words [S. Hopkins and M. Rubey, Selecta Math. (N.S.) 28 (2022), Paper No. 10] is indeed a polynomial with non-negative integer coefficients.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nA positivity conjecture for a quotient of $q$-binomial coefficients (https://arxiv.org/abs/2502.06032)\nciteturn7academia13 [wordlim: 200] Published: 1.7 years ago; Title: A positivity conjecture for a quotient of $q$-binomial coefficients\nAuthors: Mona Gatzweiler, Christian Krattenthaler ... As a corollary we obtain that a polynomial that is conjectured to be a cyclic sieving polynomial for Kreweras words [S.\nTitle: A positivity conjecture for a quotient of $q$-binomial coefficients\nAuthors: Mona Gatzweiler, Christian Krattenthaler\nDate: Sun Feb  9 21:14:05 2025\n\nWe conjecture that, if the quotient of two $q$-binomial coefficients with the same top argument is a polynomial, then it has non-negative coefficients. We summarise what is known about the conjecture and prove it in two non-trivial cases. Moreover, we move ahead to extend our conjecture to D. Stanton's fake Gaussian sequences. As a corollary we obtain that a polynomial that is conjectured to be a cyclic sieving polynomial for Kreweras words [S. Hopkins and M. Rubey, Selecta Math. (N.S.) 28 (2022), Paper No. 10] is indeed a polynomial with non-negative integer coefficients.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nBounded-orbit lattice representations of finite groups | alphaXiv (https://www.alphaxiv.org/abs/2609.35191)\nciteturn7search1 [wordlim: 200] Published: last month; Crawled: last month; @misc{du2026boundedorbitlatticerepresentationsfinite, title={Bounded-orbit lattice representations of finite groups}, author={JiaLi Du and Andrea Lucchini and Joy Morris and Pablo Spiga}, year={2026}, eprint={2609.35191}, archivePrefix={arXiv}, primaryClass={math.GR}, url={https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.35191}, }\nSubmitted 28 Sept 2026\n\n# Bounded-orbit lattice representations of finite groups\n\nJD\n\nJiaLi Du\n\nAL\n\nAndrea Lucchini\n\nJM\n\nJoy Morris\n\nPS\n\nPablo Spiga\n\n## Abstract\n\nFor a finite group $G$G, let $\\lambda(G)$λ(G) denote the minimum number of orbits on the elements of a finite lattice $L$L with $\\operatorname{Aut}(L)\\cong G$Aut(L)≅G. Babai and Goodman conjectured that $\\lambda(G)$λ(G) is bounded by an absolute constant. We prove that $\\lambda(G)\\leq 50$λ(G)≤50 for every finite group $G$G, thereby confirming their conjecture. Moreover, the lattice can be chosen to have a regular orbit. The main algebraic ingredient is a decomposition of a generating set of an arbitrary finite $2$2-group into an elementary abelian part and two sets in which no quotient of distinct elements is an involution.\n\nView PaperPDF\n\n1\n\nCite\n\n## Citation\n\nCopy\n\n@misc{du2026boundedorbitlatticerepresentationsfinite, title={Bounded-orbit lattice representations of finite groups}, author={JiaLi Du and Andrea Lucchini and Joy Morris and Pablo Spiga}, year={2026}, eprint={2609.35191}, archivePrefix={arXiv}, primaryClass={math.GR}, url={https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.35191}, }--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nImmersions and Albertson’s Conjecture (https://drops.dagstuhl.de/storage/00lipics/lipics-vol332-socg2025/html/LIPIcs.SoCG.2025.50/LIPIcs.SoCG.2025.50.html)\nciteturn7search2 [wordlim: 200] Published: 1.4 years ago; Crawled: 1.1 years ago; In 1989, Lescure and Meyniel made the following conjecture related to Hadwiger’s conjecture: Every graph of chromatic number $k$ contains ${K}_{k}$ as a weak immersion. ... DeVos, Kawarabayashi, Mohar, and Okamura [11] verified Conjecture 1 for $4\\le k\\le 6$.\n\n# Immersions and Albertson’s Conjecture\n\nJacob Fox jacobfox@stanford.edu Department of Mathematics, Stanford University, CA, USA János Pach pach@cims.nyu.edu HUN-REN Alfréd Rényi Institute of Mathematics, Budapest, Hungary Andrew Suk asuk@ucsd.edu Department of Mathematics, University of California San Diego, La Jolla, CA, USA\n\n###### Abstract\n\nA graph is said to contain ${K}_{k}$ (a clique of size $k$) as a weak immersion if it has $k$ vertices, pairwise connected by edge-disjoint paths. In 1989, Lescure and Meyniel made the following conjecture related to Hadwiger’s conjecture: Every graph of chromatic number $k$ contains ${K}_{k}$ as a weak immersion. We prove this conjecture for graphs with at most $1.4(k-1)$ vertices. As an application, we make some progress on Albertson’s conjecture on crossing numbers of graphs, according to which every graph $G$ with chromatic number $k$ satisfies $\\text{cr}(G)\\ge \\text{cr}({K}_{k})$. In particular, we show that the conjecture is true for all graphs of chromatic number $k$, provided that they have at most $1.4(k-1)$ vertices and $k$ is sufficiently large.\n\nIn 1961, Hajós conjectured the following strengthening of Hadwiger’s conjecture: Every graph of chromatic number $k$ contains a subdivision of the complete graph ${K}_{k}$, i.e., it has $k$ so-called “branch vertices” connected by $\\left(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{k}{2}\\right)$ internally vertex-disjoint paths. Hajós’ conjecture is true for $k\\le 4$, but for $k\\ge 7$ it was disproved by Catlin [8]. In fact, Erdős and Fajtlowicz [13] showed that almost all graphs are counterexamples (see also [14]). The conjecture remains open for $k=5,6$.\n\nLescure and Meyniel [19] suggested a conjecture weaker than Hajós’, which may still be true for every $k$. Instead of requiring that $G$ contains a subdivision of ${K}_{k}$, they wanted to prove the existence of $k$ branch vertices connected by $\\left(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{k}{2}\\right)$ edge-disjoint paths. Moreover, these paths may pass through some branch vertices other than their endpoints. They called such a subgraph of $G$ a weak immersion of ${K}_{k}$.\n\nMore precisely, a graph $G$ contains $H$ as a weak immersion if there is a mapping $\\varphi $ from $V(H)\\cup E(H)$, which maps each vertex of $H$ to a vertex in $G$ and each edge of $H$ to a path in $G$ such that\n\n  1. 1.\n\n$\\varphi (u)\\ne \\varphi (v)$, for distinct vertices $u,v\\in V(H)$;\n\n  2. 2.\n\nfor distinct edges $e,f\\in E(H)$, the paths $\\varphi (e)$ and $\\varphi (f)$ are edge-disjoint; and\n\n  3. 3.\n\nfor each edge $e=uv\\in E(H)$, $\\varphi (e)$ is a path in $G$ with endpoints $\\varphi (u)$ and $\\varphi (v)$.\n\nIf the following condition is also satisfied, we say that $G$ contains $H$ as a strong immersion.\n\n  1. 4.\n\nFor each edge $e\\in E(H)$, the path $\\varphi (e)$ intersects the set of branch vertices, $\\varphi (V(H))$, only at its endpoints.\n\nIn 1989, Lescure and Meyniel conjectured the following.\n\n###### Conjecture 1 ([19]).\n\nEvery graph with chromatic number $k$ contains a weak immersion of the complete graph ${K}_{k}$.\n\nFor $k\\ge 3,$ the Lescure-Meyniel conjecture is an immediate corollary of Hajós’ conjecture. DeVos, Kawarabayashi, Mohar, and Okamura [11] verified Conjecture 1 for $4\\le k\\le 6$. Conjecture 1 remains open for $k\\ge 7$. According to a result of Gauthier, Le, and Wollan [16], every graph with chromatic number $k$ contains a weak immersion of ${K}_{t}$, where $t=(k-4)/3.54$ (see also [10, 12] for earlier bounds).\n\nOur first result shows that the Lescure-Meyniel conjecture is true for graphs whose number of vertices is not much larger than its clique number.\n\n###### Theorem 2.\n\nIf $G$ is a graph with chromatic number $k$ and at most $1.4(k-1)$ vertices, then $G$ contains ${K}_{k}$ as a weak immersion.\n\nAs an application, we use Theorem 2 to obtain new bounds on an old conjecture of Albertson.\n\nThe crossing number of a graph $G$, $\\text{cr}(G)$, is the smallest number of edge crossings in any drawing of $G$ in the plane. In 2007, Albertson conjectured the following.\n\n###### Conjecture 3.\n\nEvery graph $G$ with chromatic number $k$ satisfies $\\text{cr}(G)\\ge \\text{cr}({K}_{k})$.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nCYCLIC SIEVING PHENOMENA FOR (https://www.mat.univie.ac.at/~kratt/artikel/treecycl.pdf)\nciteturn7search14 [wordlim: 200] Published: 7 months ago; Gatzweiler and C. Krattenthaler. A positivity conjecture for a quotient of q-binomial coeﬃ- ... Counting orbits under Kreweras complementation.\nHofbauer. q-Catalan numbers. J. Combin. Theory Ser. A, 40(2):248–264, 1985.\n[doi].\n[13] G. Gasper and M. Rahman. Basic hypergeometric series, volume 96 of Encyclopedia of Mathe-\nmatics and its Applications. Cambridge University Press, Cambridge, second edition, 2004. With\na foreword by Richard Askey.\n[14] M. Gatzweiler and C. Krattenthaler. A positivity conjecture for a quotient of q-binomial coeﬃ-\ncients. Ramanujan J., 69(1):13, 2026.\n[15] C. Heitsch. Counting orbits under Kreweras complementation. Preprint; arXiv:2303.12240.\n[16] S. Hopkins and M. Rubey. Promotion of Kreweras words. Selecta Math. (N.S.), 28(1):Paper No.\n10, 38, 2022. [doi].\n[17] C. Krattenthaler. Non-crossing partitions on an annulus. In preparation.\n[18] C. Krattenthaler. Counting lattice paths with a linear boundary. II. q-ballot and q-Catalan num-\nbers. Österreich. Akad. Wiss. Math.-Natur. Kl. Sitzungsber. II, 198(4–7):171–199, 1989.\n[19] C. Krattenthaler and T. W. Müller. Cyclic sieving for generalised non-crossing partitions asso-\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\narXiv Troller (https://arxiv-troller.com/?q=paper%3A+2603.10510)\nciteturn7search3 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Submitted: Oct 2026 | Updated: Nov 2026\n\nWe prove that every graph $G$ with independence number at most two contains a strong immersion of $K_{χ(G)}$. This strengthens Dahlke's theorem for weak immersions and establishes the Lescure--Meyniel conjecture for this class of graphs. ... For an integer \\(k\\ge 3\\), let \\(T_k(3)\\) be the least positive integer \\(N\\) such that, for any \\(3\\)-coloring of \\([1,N]\\), there exists a monochromatic solution to \\(x+(k-1)y=z\\). In this paper, we prove that \\(T_k(3)=k^3+2k^2-2\\) for all \\(k\\ge 3\\).\n\nStrong complete immersions in graphs with independence number two\n\nZijian Deng, Chuanshu Wu\n\narXiv:2611.00956\n\nAuthors: Zijian Deng, Chuanshu Wu\n\nSubmitted: Oct 2026 | Updated: Nov 2026\n\nWe prove that every graph $G$ with independence number at most two contains a strong immersion of $K_{χ(G)}$. This strengthens Dahlke's theorem for weak immersions and establishes the Lescure--Meyniel conjecture for this class of graphs.\n\nThe Three-Color Generalized Schur Number of \\(x+(k-1)y=z\\): A Proof for All \\(k\\) from a Critical Set of 33 Polynomials\n\nAmir Khamseh, Mohammad Hassan Safareh\n\narXiv:2611.01714\n\nAuthors: Amir Khamseh, Mohammad Hassan Safareh\n\nSubmitted: Nov 2026 | Updated: Nov 2026\n\nFor an integer \\(k\\ge 3\\), let \\(T_k(3)\\) be the least positive integer \\(N\\) such that, for any \\(3\\)-coloring of \\([1,N]\\), there exists a monochromatic solution to \\(x+(k-1)y=z\\). In this paper, we prove that \\(T_k(3)=k^3+2k^2-2\\) for all \\(k\\ge 3\\). In the notation \\(s_a(3)\\) of the first author, with \\(a=k-1\\), this says that \\(s_a(3)=a^3+5a^2+7a+1\\) for all \\(a\\ge 2\\), which confirms the conjecture proposed in 2020. --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nStrong orientations, and a nice proof of Robbins' theorem - Codeforces (https://codeforces.com/blog/entry/153338)\nciteturn7search4 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: yesterday; > In graph theory, a strong orientation of an undirected graph is an assignment of a direction to each edge that makes it into a strongly connected graph.\n\nA natural question that follows: which undirected graphs have a strong orientation? ... Robbins' theorem states that the set of graphs with strong orientations is precisely the set of bridgeless graphs.\n\n### shsh's blog\n\nStrong orientations, and a nice proof of Robbins' theorem\n\nBy shsh, history, 6 months ago, Image: In English\n\nNOTE: As always, you can also read this on my blog!\n\n## Introduction\n\nPer Wikipedia:\n\n> In graph theory, a strong orientation of an undirected graph is an assignment of a direction to each edge that makes it into a strongly connected graph.\n\nA natural question that follows: which undirected graphs have a strong orientation?\n\n## Robbins' theorem\n\nRobbins' theorem states that the set of graphs with strong orientations is precisely the set of bridgeless graphs.\n\nLemma. This condition is necessary (i.e. any graph with a bridge cannot have a strong orientation).\n\nProof. This picture should make the argument clear:\n\nImage: Bridge condition\n\nIf the edge is directed from $$$S \\to T$$$, then no node in $$$T$$$ can reach any node in $$$S$$$. Symmetric for $$$T \\to S$$$.\n\nWe now prove the more difficult half of the theorem:\n\nLemma. This condition is sufficient (i.e. any bridgeless graph has a strong orientation).\n\nProof. Robbins' original proof of his theorem introduces a tool called ear decomposition; for more on that, you can check out this Codeforces blog.\n\nToday, I'll present another approach which Wikipedia cites as Boesch and Tindell's. The core claim is as follows:\n\n> Consider a mixed graph $$$G$$$ (i.e. with both directed and undirected edges) such that every pair of nodes in $$$G$$$ can reach one another. If there exists an undirected edge $$$(u, v)$$$ in $$$G$$$ that is not a bridge, we always assign a direction to this edge such that all-pairs connectivity is preserved.\n\nIf this is true, we can direct all the edges of a bridgeless graph in any order until the entire graph is an SCC.\n\nImage: Mixed graph examples\n\nTwo examples of such mixed graphs, and the corresponding orientation (shown in blue) assigned to the edge $$$(u, v)$$$.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nPapers from 2026-05-27 - arXiv Combinatorics (https://www.arxiv.symmetricfunctions.com/date/2026-05-27)\nciteturn7search5 [wordlim: 200] Published: 5 months ago; Crawled: today; 2026-05-27 ... The Abu-Khzam--Langston conjecture, that is the weak-immersion analogue of Hadwiger's conjecture and a weak version of an earlier conjecture of Lescure and Meyniel, asserts that every graph $G$ contains a weak immersion of $K_{χ(G)}$. ... contain graph independence number coloring\n\n2026-05-27\n\nJonathan C. Dahlke\n\n# • INFO • BIB • PDF • ARXIV •\n\nThe Abu-Khzam$\\unicode{x2013}$Langston Conjecture for Graphs with $α(G) = 2$\n\nThe Abu-Khzam--Langston conjecture, that is the weak-immersion analogue of Hadwiger's conjecture and a weak version of an earlier conjecture of Lescure and Meyniel, asserts that every graph $G$ contains a weak immersion of $K_{χ(G)}$. We prove the conjecture for the class of graphs of independence number two. Along the way, we introduce a notion of \\emph{cycle-matching colouring} of a graph, a relaxation of edge-colouring in which colour classes induce vertex-disjoint unions of edges and odd cycles, and prove a sharpening of Vizing's theorem in this setting: every multigraph admits a cycle-matching colouring with at most $Δ(G)$ colours.\n\ncontain graph independence number coloring\n\n2026-05-27\n\nXuejun Guo, Zhengyu Tao\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nSkew shapes, Ehrhart positivity, and beyond - Ferroni - 2026 - Proceedings of the London Mathematical Society - Wiley Online Library (https://londmathsoc.onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/10.1112/plms.70204)\nciteturn7search6 [wordlim: 200] Published: 2 months ago; Crawled: last week; Among other applications, we generalize a positivity statement from Schubert calculus by Fomin and Kirillov (1997) from straight shapes to skew shapes. ... In [37], Gessel and Krattenthaler found the following formula for $$\\operatorname{CPP}_{\\lambda /\\mu /d}(t)$$ as a sum of Kreweras type determinants.\n\nVolume 133, Issue 2 e70204\n\nRESEARCH ARTICLE\n\nOpen Access\n\n# Skew shapes, Ehrhart positivity, and beyond\n\nFirst published: 20 August 2026\n\nhttps://doi.org/10.1112/plms.70204Digital Object Identifier (DOI)\n\n## Abstract\n\nA classical result by Kreweras (1965) allows one to compute the number of plane partitions of a given skew shape and bounded parts as certain determinants. We prove that these determinants expand as polynomials with nonnegative coefficients. This result can be reformulated in terms of order polynomials of cell posets of skew shapes and explains important positivity phenomena about the Ehrhart polynomials of shard polytopes, matroids, and order polytopes. Among other applications, we generalize a positivity statement from Schubert calculus by Fomin and Kirillov (1997) from straight shapes to skew shapes. We show that all shard polytopes are Ehrhart positive and, stronger, that all fence posets, including the zig-zag poset, and all circular fence posets have order polynomials with nonnegative coefficients. Note that $$\\Omega (P_{\\lambda /\\mu /d};t)=\\operatorname{CPP}_{\\lambda /\\mu /d}(t-1)$$. In [37], Gessel and Krattenthaler found the following formula for $$\\operatorname{CPP}_{\\lambda /\\mu /d}(t)$$ as a sum of Kreweras type determinants.\n\nTheorem 7.4. (Gessel–Krattenthaler [[37], Theorem 2])For a cylindric skew shape $$\\lambda /\\mu /d$$ with sequences of nonnegative integers $$\\lambda$$ and $$\\mu$$ satisfying (7.1) we have that\n\n$$$$\\begin{equation} \\Omega (P_{\\lambda /\\mu /d};t) = \\sum _{(k_1,\\ldots,k_{\\ell })} \\det {\\left[ \\binom{t-1 + \\lambda _i-\\mu _j -dk_i }{\\lambda _i-\\mu _j-i+j-(\\ell +d)k_i} \\right]}_{i,j=1}^{\\ell }, \\end{equation}$$$$(7.2)\n\nwhere the sum is over tuples of integers $$(k_1,\\ldots,k_{\\ell })$$ such that $$k_1+\\cdots + k_{\\ell }=0$$.\n\nRemark 7.5.The number of terms in the RHS of (7.2) is finite because, in order to have a nonvanishing determinant, in every row of the matrix we need to have at least one nonzero entry. Thus, for $$i=1,\\ldots,\\ell$$ we have that $$k_i \\leqslant (\\lambda _i-\\mu _j -i+j)/(\\ell +d)$$ for some $$j$$ and subsequently that $$k_i \\geqslant -(\\ell -1)(\\lambda _i-\\mu _j -i+j)/(\\ell +d)$$ for some $$j$$.\n\nWhen the skew shape $$\\lambda /\\mu$$ is a ribbon or border strip and $$d=\\lambda _1-1$$, the shape $$\\lambda /\\mu /d$$ is called a closed ribbon, and the poset $$P_{\\lambda /\\mu /d}$$ is called circular fence poset, see Figure 8 for an example. These posets played an instrumental role in the proof by Oğuz and Ravichandran [71] mentioned in Section 5.1.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nList of C. Krattenthaler's papers (https://www.mat.univie.ac.at/~kratt/papers.html)\nciteturn7search7 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: last month; Chan), Recent progress in the study of representations of integers as sums of squares, Bull. ... A Gog-Magog conjecture, 5 pages, unpublished manuscript, 1996; updated 2016. ... Krattenthaler, G.\nSagan†www.mth.msu.edu】, R. P. Stanley, eds., Progress in Math., vol. 161, Birkhäuser, Boston, 1998, pp. 263-270.\n\n44. A new proof of the M-R-R conjecture - including a generalization, J. Difference Equ. Appl. 5 (1999), 335-351.\n\n45. (with P. Slater), Asymptotic Redundancies for Universal Quantum Coding, IEEE Trans. Information Theory 46 (2000), 801-819.\n\n46. (with D. Zeilberger), Proof of a determinant evaluation conjectured by Bombieri, Hunt and van der Poorten, New York J. Math. 3 (1997), 54-102.\n\n47. Another involution principle-free bijective proof of Stanley's hook-content formula, J. Combin. Theory Ser. A 88 (1999), 66-92.\n\n48. (with S. Okada), The number of rhombus tilings of a \"punctured\" hexagon and the minor summation formula, 【72†Adv. # Other articles\n\nA1. Product formulae for trace generating functions for semistandard tableaux of unusual shapes, 5 pages, unpublished manuscript, 199